The '''Balboa Canal Authority''' ({{lang|ca|Autoritat del Canal de Balboa}}) is a [[Balboa|Balboan]] state agency responsible for the sovereign governance of the [[Balboa Canal]]. It was established by the [[Treaty of Areza]] (1973) upon the return of the canal to Balboan sovereignty, and operates under the authority of the [[Ministry of Interior (Balboa)|Ministry of Interior]]. The Authority sets transit policy, determines toll rates, collects canal tolls, and ensures compliance with the treaty's non-discrimination and access provisions. Day-to-day operational services — including pilotage, towage, berthing, and infrastructure maintenance — are provided by [[L'empresa del Canal i el Port]] (ECP) under concession to the Authority.

== History ==

=== Establishment ===

The Balboa Canal Authority was established in 1973 by the [[Treaty of Areza]], which returned the canal to Balboan sovereignty after the [[Adonian Community]] mandate period (1950–1973). The treaty designated the canal as an international waterway, open to the vessels of all nations on non-discriminatory terms, and created the Authority as the Balboan state entity responsible for upholding those obligations.

The Authority replaced the [[AC Canal Commission]]'s direct operational role, though the Commission retained oversight authority under the treaty. The transfer of sovereignty was gradual: the AC Commission continued to supervise the Authority's early operations, and the transition to full Balboan control was not completed until the early 1980s.

=== Ministry of Interior administration ===

From its establishment, the Authority has been administered through the [[Ministry of Interior (Balboa)|Ministry of Interior]]. The placement reflected the canal's strategic importance to the Balboan state — the waterway was the country's most valuable asset, and the government regarded its control as a matter of national security as much as commercial policy. The Ministry of Interior, which also retained responsibility for canal defence and security, was the natural institutional home for the Authority.

During the first decade of Balboan sovereignty (1973–1986), the Authority operated the canal's services directly through the Ministry of Interior. This arrangement proved cumbersome: the ministry's bureaucratic structure was poorly suited to the commercial demands of a growing international port, and the canal's infrastructure was deteriorating for lack of investment. These limitations led to the 1986 spin-off of port and canal service operations into [[L'empresa del Canal i el Port]] (ECP), a public-private partnership that took over operational services under concession to the Authority.

=== Modern era ===

Since the ECP spin-off, the Authority has focused on its sovereign functions: toll collection, transit policy, treaty compliance, and strategic oversight of the canal's long-term capacity and infrastructure. The Authority sets the terms under which ECP operates, approves major capital works, and retains the right to intervene in operational matters that affect treaty compliance or national security.

== Responsibilities ==

The Authority's responsibilities are defined by the Treaty of Areza and by Balboan domestic law. They include:

* '''Toll collection''': The Authority sets toll rates, calculates charges on the basis of vessel tonnage and transit type, and collects all canal tolls. Toll revenue — approximately $6.5 billion annually — flows directly to the Balboan treasury, not through ECP.
* '''Transit policy''': The Authority determines the rules governing canal transit, including scheduling, priority systems, vessel size limits, and safety requirements.
* '''Treaty compliance''': The Authority is responsible for ensuring that the canal remains open to the vessels of all nations on non-discriminatory terms, as required by the Treaty of Areza. It must not grant preferential access to any state or use the canal as an instrument of commercial or diplomatic leverage.
* '''Strategic oversight''': The Authority approves major capital works, expansion programmes, and long-term infrastructure plans. It sets the framework within which ECP operates and maintains the canal's physical infrastructure.
* '''Defence and security''': Through the Ministry of Interior, the Authority retains responsibility for the physical security of the canal zone and the defence of the waterway and its facilities.

== Relationship with ECP ==

{{main|L'empresa del Canal i el Port}}

[[L'empresa del Canal i el Port]] (ECP) operates the canal's service infrastructure under concession to the Authority. The division of responsibilities is:

* The '''Authority''' sets toll rates, collects tolls, determines transit policy, ensures treaty compliance, and retains sovereign control over the canal.
* '''ECP''' provides operational services — pilotage, towage, berthing, mooring, cargo handling, vessel servicing, and infrastructure maintenance — and pays an annual concession rent to the government.

The two entities work in close operational coordination but maintain separate governance and financial structures. The Authority does not set ECP's service fees or manage its commercial operations; ECP does not set toll policy or collect transit fees.

== Relationship with the AC Canal Commission ==

{{main|AC Canal Commission}}

The [[AC Canal Commission]], established by the Treaty of Areza, retains oversight authority over the canal. The Commission — consisting of representatives of the [[Adonian Community]], the Republic of Balboa, and a rotating panel of five major maritime nations — monitors compliance with the treaty's provisions and adjudicates disputes regarding access and tolls. The Commission's authority supersedes Balboan domestic law within the canal zone, which extends approximately 10 kilometres on either side of the waterway.

The Authority is required to report regularly to the Commission on toll revenue, transit volumes, compliance matters, and infrastructure conditions. The Commission may direct the Authority to adjust toll rates or transit policies if it finds that the treaty's non-discrimination provisions have been violated.

== Governance ==

The Authority is headed by a Director-General appointed by the [[Ministry of Interior (Balboa)|Ministry of Interior]], with the approval of the [[National Assembly of Balboa|National Assembly]]. The Director-General serves a fixed term and reports to the Minister of Interior. The Authority's budget is funded from canal toll revenue, allocated through the national budget process.

